 and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072378/assets/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=5y-x+3$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=5y-x+3$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072378/assets/ffa57d3e7fed08212f12d4b9d7c2e0a4d33a3aac71c8b8a03d1a9a7364484deb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072378/assets/c4aa9e96fe72a8fb31ab62ff7eb0695cc64992bb9433be516461915a5c92baff.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j-1})=5{y}_{j-1}-{x}_{i}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(5{y}_{j-1}-{x}_{i}+3)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $5{y}_{j-1}-{x}_{i}+3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41a9d47a-2dd6-5177-afd1-e4adcd887f55" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "349a8206-d33e-5714-9ce2-1cb16a0b7919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}_{i-1}^{2}+7{y}_{j-1}^{2}-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376743} {:answer/id #uuid "6f54804f-d6d9-5520-8733-e09934156a6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}_{i-1}^{2}+4{y}_{j-1}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 175