{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072378/assets/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=5x+8y+11$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=5x+8y+11$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072378/assets/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072378/assets/51808df96cf7dd295bfa24aa3641a601c821116e1a0014898ffe705036704a53.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=5{x}_{i-1}+8{y}_{j-1}+11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(5{x}_{i-1}+8{y}_{j-1}+11)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $5{x}_{i-1}+8{y}_{j-1}+11.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41a9d47a-2dd6-5177-afd1-e4adcd887f55" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "667e9204-a5cd-513c-8f12-f93080452a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11{x}_{i-1}+2{y}_{j}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 175